const MBBS_TOPIC_RAW = {};
MBBS_TOPIC_RAW.ophthalmology = `
anatomy~Eye anatomy and the visual pathway~Structure and examination~0~Light enters through the clear cornea, passes through the pupil and lens, and reaches the retina.|Retinal cells convert light into signals that travel through the optic nerve and further brain pathways.|A problem can be optical, retinal, or neural, so locating the affected stage organizes the explanation.~Cornea and lens focus; retina detects; pathways carry and process.|Seeing requires more than clear optical surfaces.~Why can an eye with a clear lens still have poor vision?~Retinal cells, optic nerves, or later visual pathways may be affected even when the optical path is clear.~Draw light entry and the separate nerve-signal route to the brain.
refraction~Refraction, myopia, hyperopia and accommodation~Structure and examination~0~Refraction bends light at boundaries between materials. The eye's shape and focusing surfaces determine where light converges.|Myopia focuses distant parallel rays in front of the retina; hyperopia focuses them behind it in an unaccommodated eye.|Accommodation changes lens focusing power for near vision and must be distinguished from moving the whole eye.~Focus location explains refractive blur.|Accommodation and pupil size are different responses.~Why does a refractive error not automatically imply retinal disease?~The light may be focused incorrectly despite a functioning retina, so optical focus and tissue health are separate questions.~Draw focus in front of, on, and behind the retina.
examination~Visual acuity, fields, pupils and examination logic~Structure and examination~0~Visual acuity measures detail resolution under specified conditions. Visual fields measure the area seen while gaze remains directed.|Pupil responses and eye movements examine other parts of the visual and motor systems.|An examination combines these questions; one normal result cannot confirm every component of vision.~Acuity and field are different functions.|Examination findings need the patient's symptoms and timing.~Why can a person read a chart yet have important visual-field loss?~Central detail resolution can remain useful while other regions of the visual field are impaired.~List what acuity, field, pupil, and movement tests each investigate.
lids-lacrimal~Eyelids, tear film and lacrimal drainage~Ocular surface~1~Eyelids protect the eye and spread the tear film during blinking.|The tear system maintains a suitable surface and drains fluid through a route toward the nose. Production, surface retention, and drainage are distinct steps.|Watery eyes can reflect increased production or impaired drainage, so the visible result alone does not identify the mechanism.~Tears support the optical surface as well as comfort.|Overflow is not always excess production.~Why can a blocked drainage route cause watering despite ordinary tear production?~Fluid accumulates because its normal exit pathway is impaired, producing overflow.~Draw tear production, spreading, and drainage as a simple route.
conjunctiva~Conjunctivitis and red-eye reasoning~Ocular surface~1~The conjunctiva is a lining over parts of the eye surface and inner eyelids. Its inflammation can produce redness and irritation.|Redness can also arise from corneal, intraocular, or other disease; pain, light sensitivity, and reduced vision change the assessment.|A tissue-based explanation helps avoid labeling every red eye as uncomplicated conjunctivitis.~Red eye is a presentation, not a single diagnosis.|Locate the inflamed structure.~Why is reduced vision important when interpreting a red eye?~It raises concern that structures involved in optical clarity or visual function may be affected, requiring broader assessment.~Make a red-eye comparison organized by tissue and effect on vision.
cornea~Corneal transparency, keratitis and ulceration~Ocular surface~1~The cornea must be clear and appropriately shaped to transmit and focus light.|Inflammation or tissue loss can disrupt its surface, structure, and transparency. Infection and noninfectious injury are different possible causes.|Scarring can change light transmission even after active injury has settled.~Transparency and curvature both matter.|A surface injury can have a lasting optical effect.~Why can a healed central corneal scar impair sight?~It may scatter or distort incoming light at a key part of the optical path even though the surface has closed.~Draw normal cornea beside a scarred region and show altered light passage.
lens-cataract~Lens function and cataract~Lens and pressure-related disease~2~The lens helps focus light and changes shape during accommodation.|Cataract is loss of lens transparency, causing light scattering and visual disturbance. Symptoms must be related to the rest of the eye examination.|A lens opacity and other eye disease can coexist, so one visible abnormality need not explain all symptoms.~Cataract is within the lens.|Clearer optics cannot correct every neural cause of vision loss.~Why should the retina and optic nerve be considered when assessing cataract-related symptoms?~They also determine vision, and coexisting disease may contribute to impairment beyond the lens opacity.~Trace how lens scattering differs from damage to the retinal signal pathway.
glaucoma~Glaucoma, aqueous flow and optic-nerve damage~Lens and pressure-related disease~3~Fluid production and drainage influence pressure within the eye.|Glaucoma involves characteristic optic-nerve damage and related functional loss; pressure is an important factor but not the whole definition.|A pressure reading needs interpretation alongside nerve appearance, field findings, and clinical context.~Pressure is a risk-related measurement.|Glaucoma is an optic neuropathy.~Why does a normal pressure reading not exclude glaucoma?~Characteristic nerve damage can occur without an elevated reading at that assessment, and one measurement does not describe the entire disease process.~Draw fluid flow separately from the optic-nerve and visual-field relationship.
uveitis~Uveal inflammation and systemic connections~Internal eye and neural disease~3~The uvea is the eye's middle vascular layer, including iris, ciliary body, and choroid.|Inflammation in this region can have infectious, immune-related, or other causes, with different patterns by location.|Eye findings can be linked with systemic conditions, but the association requires appropriate assessment rather than assumption.~Uveitis names a tissue process, not one cause.|Location within the eye changes the presentation.~Why is naming uveitis only the beginning of an explanation?~It identifies where inflammation occurs but not its cause, extent, or consequences for visual function.~Map anterior and posterior eye structures and locate possible inflammatory effects.
retina~Retinal vascular disease, diabetes and detachment~Internal eye and neural disease~4~The retina is living neural tissue requiring reliable supply and structural support.|Diabetes and hypertension can affect retinal vessels. Retinal detachment separates the light-sensitive layer from its underlying support.|These mechanisms differ even though both can impair vision, so a symptom alone does not identify the lesion.~Vessel damage and layer separation are different processes.|Sudden visual changes differ from slow progression.~Why can systemic vascular disease affect the eye?~Retinal tissue depends on small vessels that can share the effects of broader metabolic and pressure-related disease.~Draw retinal blood supply and supporting layers, then mark two different injury routes.
neuro-strabismus~Optic pathways, eye movements and strabismus~Internal eye and neural disease~5~The optic pathways carry organized visual information to the brain; the pattern of field loss can help locate a lesion.|Eye alignment depends on muscles, nerves, and coordinated control. Strabismus means misalignment, not simply poor focus.|Sensory vision and motor alignment interact, but they are not identical functions.~Visual-field pattern and eye-movement pattern answer different localization questions.|Misalignment is not the same as refractive error.~Why can an eye-movement nerve problem cause double vision despite clear lenses?~The eyes may point in different directions, sending mismatched images even when each optical pathway remains clear.~Draw two aligned visual axes and then illustrate a misaligned pair.
trauma-community~Eye trauma, avoidable visual loss and practical study~Applied ophthalmology~5~Eye injury can affect surface, internal structures, or the visual pathways. External appearance alone may underestimate severity.|Community eye health addresses prevention, access, detection, and rehabilitation as well as treatment of individual diseases.|Practical learning requires supervised examination and recognition of findings, not only reading condition names.~Structure, function, and urgency must be considered together.|Population prevention and individual diagnosis are different tasks.~Why can preventing exposure be as important as treating eye injury?~Avoiding the injury preserves structures that may not fully recover even with later care.~Prepare a supervised eye-examination study checklist organized by function and structure.
`;
MBBS_TOPIC_RAW.ent = `
ear-anatomy~Outer, middle and inner ear anatomy~Ear and hearing~0~The outer ear collects sound and directs it toward the eardrum. Middle-ear bones transmit vibration onward.|The cochlea converts motion into nerve signals, while nearby balance organs detect aspects of head movement.|Anatomical stages predict different types of hearing and balance disturbance.~Collection, transmission, and conversion are separate stages.|The inner ear serves hearing and balance through different structures.~Why can an outer-ear obstruction affect hearing without damaging the auditory nerve?~It reduces sound reaching the conversion system, producing a transmission problem despite an intact neural route.~Draw sound traveling from the ear canal to the auditory nerve.
hearing-tests~Conductive loss, sensorineural loss and audiometry~Ear and hearing~0~Conductive loss involves reduced sound transmission; sensorineural loss involves cochlear or neural dysfunction.|Tuning-fork tests can suggest a pattern, while audiometry measures hearing responses more systematically.|Interpretation depends on method and context, so a screening finding is not a complete anatomical diagnosis.~Transmission differs from signal conversion.|Test patterns require cross-checking.~Why does separating conductive from sensorineural patterns matter?~The patterns point to different parts of the hearing pathway and therefore different mechanisms to investigate.~Create a table linking pathway stage, loss type, and the question a hearing test asks.
external-ear~External-ear disease and wax obstruction~Ear and hearing~1~The ear canal is a skin-lined passage leading to the eardrum.|Obstruction changes sound transmission; inflammation can produce pain, swelling, or discharge. These mechanisms can overlap but are not identical.|The location of disease must be distinguished from middle-ear pathology behind the eardrum.~Ear discharge does not identify one location by itself.|The eardrum separates canal and middle-ear spaces.~Why can swelling of the canal reduce hearing?~It narrows or blocks the sound-conducting route before vibrations reach the eardrum.~Draw canal, eardrum, and middle ear and place examples of surface versus deeper disease.
otitis-media~Eustachian tube, otitis media and effusion~Ear and hearing~1~The eustachian tube links the middle ear with the upper throat and helps pressure regulation.|Poor ventilation can contribute to fluid accumulation. Acute middle-ear infection and fluid without acute infection are distinct states.|The same hearing complaint can therefore arise from related but different mechanisms.~Effusion means fluid, not necessarily active bacterial infection.|Pressure balance helps middle-ear function.~Why can middle-ear fluid reduce hearing even without nerve injury?~Fluid and altered mechanics reduce efficient vibration transmission through the middle ear.~Trace tube dysfunction to pressure change, fluid, and altered hearing.
chronic-ear~Chronic ear disease, cholesteatoma and complications~Ear and hearing~1~Persistent ear disease can damage structures over time. Cholesteatoma is abnormal keratinizing tissue that can erode nearby structures.|The middle ear lies close to facial-nerve, balance, and cranial structures, explaining why complications may extend beyond hearing.|Symptoms, examination, and appropriate investigations must be combined to determine extent.~Local disease can affect neighboring structures.|Cholesteatoma is not simply accumulated ear wax.~Why can a middle-ear process affect facial movement or balance?~Important nerve and inner-ear structures lie nearby and can be affected by extension or erosion.~Draw the middle ear and label neighboring structures relevant to complications.
vestibular~Vertigo and vestibular integration~Balance~2~Balance integrates inner-ear, visual, and body-position information.|Vertigo is a false or distorted sense of movement. It can arise from peripheral balance structures or central processing.|Associated neurological findings and timing matter; one symptom does not conclusively localize every case.~Dizziness is broader than vertigo.|Balance uses several inputs.~Why can conflicting sensory inputs produce a movement sensation while a person is still?~The brain receives inconsistent information about motion or orientation and the combined interpretation can be distorted.~Draw three sensory inputs converging on balance control.
nose-anatomy~Nasal anatomy, airflow and smell~Nose and sinuses~3~The nose filters, warms, and moistens incoming air. Its lining and shape influence resistance and flow.|Smell depends on specialized sensory cells and neural pathways, rather than airflow alone.|A blockage and a sensory-system problem can both affect perceived smell through different routes.~Air conditioning and smell detection are distinct functions.|Location and mechanism explain symptoms.~Why can nasal blockage reduce smell even when smell receptors are intact?~Odor molecules may not reach the sensory region effectively, reducing input to an otherwise functioning receptor system.~Draw an airflow path and a separate smell-signal path.
rhinosinusitis~Rhinitis, sinus drainage and nasal polyps~Nose and sinuses~3~Rhinitis is inflammation of the nasal lining and may be allergic or nonallergic.|Sinuses communicate with the nasal cavity through drainage pathways. Swelling can interfere with those routes and alter symptoms.|Duration, pattern, and examination are more informative than discharge color alone when interpreting possible infection.~Rhinitis and sinus involvement are related but distinct.|Color alone does not prove a bacterial cause.~Why can mucosal swelling cause symptoms beyond the main nasal passage?~It can narrow connected drainage openings, affecting air and fluid movement in sinus spaces.~Sketch a sinus drainage opening before and after lining swelling.
epistaxis~Epistaxis and nasal obstruction reasoning~Nose and sinuses~3~Epistaxis means nasal bleeding. Local tissue factors and systemic conditions can contribute.|Assessment distinguishes source, severity, recurrence, and associated factors rather than assuming one cause from the symptom name.|Nasal obstruction similarly needs localization and time-course information to distinguish different mechanisms.~A symptom label does not supply the cause.|Local and systemic contributors can coexist.~Why does recurrent bleeding require more information than the fact that blood came from the nose?~The source and contributing conditions vary, so recurrence, context, and examination help explain the mechanism.~Create a history framework for timing, side, triggers, associated features, and context.
pharynx~Pharynx, tonsils and swallowing disorders~Throat and airway~4~The pharynx is a shared passage involved in breathing and swallowing. Coordination directs food toward the esophagus while protecting the airway.|Tonsillar inflammation, localized collections, and noninfectious conditions can all produce throat symptoms.|Dysphagia means difficulty swallowing; odynophagia means pain on swallowing, so they should be asked about separately.~Difficulty and pain are different symptoms.|Swallowing requires coordination, not gravity alone.~Why can a person have dysphagia without odynophagia?~Movement or passage can be impaired without a painful inflammatory process, so difficulty and pain do not have to occur together.~Trace oral, pharyngeal, and esophageal phases with airway protection marked.
larynx~Larynx, voice, hoarseness and stridor~Throat and airway~5~The larynx protects the airway and contains vocal folds that vibrate to generate sound.|Voice changes can result from altered tissue, movement, or nerve control. Stridor instead points to turbulent airflow through a narrowed upper airway.|Persistent or concerning symptoms need anatomical assessment rather than assumptions from sound alone.~Hoarseness concerns voice quality.|Stridor concerns breathing through a narrowed upper airway.~Why is hoarseness different from stridor?~One describes altered voice production, while the other is a breathing sound related to narrowed airflow; they may arise through different mechanisms.~Draw airflow through the larynx and label vibration versus narrowing effects.
neck-emergencies~Neck masses, head-neck disease and practical examination~Throat and airway~5~Neck masses may arise from lymph nodes, glands, developmental structures, or other tissues.|Location, duration, associated symptoms, and examination guide further assessment; size alone does not identify the cause.|Practical ENT learning connects instrument findings with function and recognizes when airway or other urgent features change priorities.~Locate the tissue before naming the disease.|A mass is a finding, not automatically a malignancy.~Why should a neck examination consider swallowing and voice as well as the lump itself?~Neighboring passages and nerves may be affected, so functional changes help define the lesion's relationships and significance.~Prepare a supervised ENT examination checklist organized into ear, nose, throat, and neck.
`;
MBBS_TOPIC_RAW.community = `
health-determinants~Health, disease and social determinants~Population foundations~5~Health is influenced by biology, living conditions, work, income, education, and access to care.|A determinant changes the conditions in which illness develops or recovery occurs; it may act at individual or population level.|Explaining population patterns therefore requires more than listing personal habits.~Individual choices operate within social conditions.|A determinant changes probability, not every person's destiny.~Why can the same health advice have different practical value for two households?~Their resources, housing, work demands, and access may differ, changing whether the advice can realistically be followed.~Map one health problem across individual, household, community, and service factors.
indicators~Incidence, prevalence, rates and ratios~Population foundations~0~Incidence describes new cases over a defined time, while prevalence describes existing cases at a point or period.|Rates, proportions, and ratios use different reference quantities. Their numerator, denominator, population, and time must be explicit.|A changing count can reflect a larger population rather than a changing individual risk.~Count alone is not a rate.|Existing and new cases answer different questions.~Why can prevalence rise without an increase in new-case risk?~People may live longer with the condition, increasing the number of existing cases even if incidence does not rise.~Create a small population example distinguishing new cases from all existing cases.
study-design~Descriptive studies, cohorts, case-control and trials~Epidemiology and evidence~0~Descriptive studies identify patterns by person, place, and time. Analytical designs compare exposures and outcomes.|Cohorts follow groups toward outcomes; case-control studies begin with outcome groups and compare past exposures. Trials assign interventions under a protocol.|Design determines strengths and limits, especially for timing, confounding, and causal inference.~Ask how participants were selected.|Observational association alone is not proof of causation.~Why is exposure timing easier to establish in a prospective cohort than a one-time snapshot?~Exposure is documented before later outcomes are observed, supporting a clearer time sequence, though other biases may still remain.~Draw participant selection and observation direction for three designs.
bias-causation~Bias, confounding and causal reasoning~Epidemiology and evidence~0~Bias is systematic error, while chance is random variation. Confounding occurs when another factor distorts an exposure-outcome comparison.|A large sample may reduce random uncertainty but does not automatically remove poor measurement or biased selection.|Causal reasoning examines design, timing, alternative explanations, and the broader evidence.~More data cannot repair every design flaw.|A confounder relates to the comparison's exposure and outcome structure.~Why can a precise estimate still be wrong?~A systematic error can shift results consistently, giving a narrow uncertainty interval around a biased value.~Describe a hypothetical confounder and how it could distort a comparison.
statistics~Data summaries, variability and uncertainty~Epidemiology and evidence~1~The mean, median, and mode summarize different aspects of data. Extreme values strongly influence the mean.|Measures of spread show how much observations differ. An estimate without uncertainty can look more definite than the data justify.|Statistical significance does not by itself establish clinical importance or eliminate bias.~Center and spread belong together.|A p-value is not the probability that a hypothesis is true.~For 1, 2, 3, 4, and 40, why does the median better resist the extreme value?~The median is the middle ordered observation, 3, while the mean uses all magnitudes and is pulled upward to 10.~Calculate mean and median before and after adding one extreme observation.
screening~Screening, sensitivity and predictive values~Epidemiology and evidence~2~Screening seeks possible disease in a target group; confirmation and management are separate next steps.|Sensitivity is positive among people with disease, while specificity is negative among those without disease.|Predictive values concern the meaning of test results in the tested population and therefore depend on prevalence as well as test performance.~Always state the denominator.|A positive screen is not automatically a diagnosis.~If 80 of 100 people with disease test positive, what is sensitivity?~Sensitivity is 80/100, or 80%. The example does not provide specificity because it gives no results for people without disease.~Draw a two-by-two table and label each test measure's denominator.
surveillance-outbreak~Surveillance and outbreak investigation~Communicable disease control~3~Surveillance systematically collects, analyzes, and uses health information for action.|An apparent outbreak needs a clear case definition, comparison with expected occurrence, and analysis by time, place, and person.|Investigation and proportionate control can proceed together, with conclusions updated as evidence improves.~A case definition makes counting consistent.|A cluster is a signal to investigate, not proof of one cause.~Why can changing a case definition change an epidemic curve?~Different people or events may now meet the counting rule, altering apparent timing and magnitude even without a new biological event.~Create a small line list with date, place, exposure, and case-definition status.
communicable-programmes~Transmission control and communicable-disease programmes~Communicable disease control~3~Control strategies interrupt an agent's route from source to susceptible host.|Programmes combine detection, treatment access, prevention, and monitoring, with choices depending on the disease mechanism and setting.|Programme names, eligibility, and operational instructions can change, so study the enduring logic and consult current official guidance for details.~Match intervention to transmission route.|A programme needs measurable outcomes.~Why might early detection alone be insufficient to reduce transmission?~People also need effective access to care and prevention, while environmental or contact routes may continue without other measures.~Map detection, care, prevention, and monitoring for one communicable-disease programme.
immunization~Immunization principles and cold-chain concepts~Communicable disease control~3~Vaccines prepare immune responses to specific targets. Population impact depends on effective delivery and uptake as well as biological performance.|Storage and handling help preserve product quality; the cold chain is the temperature-controlled supply pathway for products requiring it.|Schedules and catch-up decisions are programme-specific and should be checked against current official guidance.~A schedule and a vaccine's mechanism answer different questions.|Availability, quality, and uptake all influence protection.~Why can a good vaccine have limited population impact if delivery is unreliable?~People may not receive effective doses at the intended times or coverage may remain too low, reducing realized protection.~Draw the supply-to-recipient chain and identify points where quality or access can fail.
ncd~Noncommunicable diseases and risk reduction~Prevention across the life course~3~Noncommunicable conditions arise through interacting biological, behavioral, environmental, and social factors.|Prevention can reduce exposure before disease, detect disease early, and limit consequences after it develops.|Risk reduction must be described with a baseline, population, and time frame rather than a relative percentage alone.~Primary, secondary, and tertiary prevention have different timing.|A risk factor does not guarantee an outcome.~Why does halving a risk require the starting risk to be meaningful?~A fall from 20% to 10% and from 2% to 1% both halve relative risk but have different absolute changes.~Calculate absolute and relative changes for two starting risks.
nutrition~Nutrition assessment and deficiency prevention~Prevention across the life course~4~Nutrition assessment combines intake, body measurements, clinical findings, and selected laboratory information.|Age, pregnancy, illness, and activity change requirements; a single weight does not describe diet quality or every deficiency.|Population interventions may act through food access, fortification, education, or targeted services depending on the problem.~Energy intake and micronutrient adequacy differ.|Interpret measurements with age and context.~Why can a community have both undernutrition and diet-related chronic disease?~Different people or even different nutrient dimensions in one person can be affected, reflecting unequal access and dietary composition.~Prepare a nutrition-assessment checklist covering diet, growth or body size, and context.
maternal-child~Maternal, newborn and child health indicators~Prevention across the life course~4~Maternal and child health follows a continuum before pregnancy, during pregnancy, around birth, and through childhood.|Indicators summarize outcomes and service access, but definitions and denominators must match the event being counted.|Differences in outcomes can reflect care, nutrition, social conditions, and data quality together.~An indicator describes a defined population event.|Continuity matters across stages of care.~Why is comparing raw infant-death counts between districts misleading?~Districts may have very different numbers of births, so counts need an appropriate denominator before comparing risk.~Draw a care continuum and add one measurement question at each stage.
demography-family~Demography, family planning and population change~Population and services~4~Population size changes through births, deaths, and migration. Age structure affects service needs even when total size is unchanged.|Family-planning services support informed reproductive choices using suitable information and access.|Population measures should be interpreted without reducing people to targets or ignoring voluntary decision-making.~Population size and age distribution are different.|Reproductive choices require informed, voluntary participation.~Why can an aging population need different services without growing in size?~A larger proportion of older people changes the distribution of health needs even if the total count remains stable.~Compare service needs for two hypothetical age distributions.
environment~Water, sanitation, air and waste~Population and services~5~Environmental health reduces harmful exposure through safe systems, not only treatment after illness.|Water safety includes protecting sources, appropriate treatment, and reliable distribution. Sanitation and waste management interrupt other exposure routes.|A failure at one stage can undermine an otherwise well-designed system.~Source, pathway, and recipient form an exposure chain.|Treatment alone cannot guarantee safe distribution.~Why can treated water become unsafe before use?~Contamination can enter during storage or distribution, so protection must continue after the treatment step.~Map a water supply from source to household and mark potential contamination points.
occupational~Occupational health and exposure control~Population and services~5~A workplace hazard is a potential source of harm; risk depends on exposure and protective conditions.|Controls can eliminate or substitute a hazard, change engineering conditions, organize work differently, or protect individuals.|Reducing exposure at its source is different from depending entirely on each worker's protective behavior.~Hazard and exposure are different concepts.|Upstream control can protect many people together.~Why is protective equipment alone often a less reliable control than removing a hazard?~Its success depends on correct availability, fit, use, and maintenance, while removal prevents the exposure route more directly.~Arrange example controls from source elimination to personal protection and explain the order.
health-systems~Primary care, health education, research and field practice~Population and services~5~Health systems connect prevention, treatment, rehabilitation, and continuing support across service levels.|Communication must fit people's language, priorities, and resources. Research and field work require clear questions, respectful methods, and reliable records.|Family and community visits build longitudinal understanding; they are not simply opportunities to collect isolated measurements.~Services need continuity and referral links.|A field observation needs context and consent.~Why can a technically correct health message fail to change behavior?~It may be unclear, impractical, or disconnected from the person's priorities and available resources.~Design a supervised field-study outline with question, consent, observations, feedback, and follow-up.
`;
MBBS_TOPIC_RAW.forensic = `
ethics-consent~Ethics, consent, capacity and confidentiality~Professional practice~0~Valid decision-making requires understandable information, relevant capacity, and freedom from coercion.|Confidentiality protects private information within applicable legal and professional requirements.|An ethical analysis identifies the decision, the affected person, the relevant duties, and the reasons for a course of action.~A signature is not the whole consent process.|Capacity concerns the specific decision and context.~Why does a signed form not prove informed consent?~A person may have signed without understanding, sufficient information, or freedom to decide, so the process must be considered.~Write a plain-language explanation of a hypothetical examination and a way to check understanding.
documentation~Medical records, reports and evidence handling~Professional practice~1~Records distinguish what was reported, what was observed, and what was concluded.|Evidence handling requires reliable identification and documentation of collection, transfer, storage, and analysis.|Chain of custody supports traceability; it does not make a poorly collected sample scientifically reliable by itself.~Observation and inference need separate wording.|Document handling history and sample limitations.~Why can a correct laboratory result still be difficult to use if the sample identity is uncertain?~The result may not be securely linked to the relevant person or event, weakening its evidential meaning.~Create a mock evidence log using fictional identifiers and transfer times.
identification~Personal identification and biological evidence~Identification and death~1~Identification can draw on fingerprints, dental records, DNA, and contextual information.|Different methods have different strengths, sample requirements, and uncertainty. Agreement between independent evidence can strengthen an assessment.|A biological match must still be interpreted with sample quality, comparison method, and the question being asked.~A finding's meaning depends on its reference comparison.|Identity and cause of death are different questions.~Why is an isolated physical resemblance weaker than a well-supported identification process?~Many people share visible features, while validated comparisons can evaluate more specific information with stated limitations.~Compare identification methods by sample, reference needed, and main limitation.
death-changes~Death, postmortem changes and time estimation~Identification and death~2~After death, cooling, blood settling, stiffness, and decomposition arise through different processes.|Environmental conditions, body factors, and circumstances influence their development.|Time estimation integrates evidence and uncertainty rather than assigning an exact universal time from one sign.~Algor: cooling; livor: settling; rigor: stiffness.|Context changes the meaning of a postmortem observation.~Why is one temperature measurement insufficient for an exact time of death?~Starting conditions, surroundings, body characteristics, and heat exchange vary, so the same measurement can fit different timelines.~Make an observation-context-limitation table for three postmortem changes.
autopsy~Autopsy principles and cause-of-death reasoning~Identification and death~2~A postmortem examination combines external findings, internal examination, sampling, and circumstantial information.|Cause of death, the physiological mechanism, and the broader circumstances are related but distinct descriptions.|A conclusion must connect findings to a defensible explanation and acknowledge unresolved alternatives.~A terminal mechanism alone may not identify the underlying cause.|Findings are interpreted together, not in isolation.~Why does saying that breathing stopped not identify the underlying cause of death?~It describes a final physiological event shared by many conditions rather than the disease or injury that initiated the sequence.~Write a fictional chain separating initiating condition, intermediate effects, and final mechanism.
injuries~Blunt, sharp and firearm injury descriptions~Injuries and environmental effects~3~Mechanical force can scrape, bruise, tear, cut, or penetrate tissue, producing different descriptive patterns.|Location, dimensions, margins, surrounding changes, and associated injuries help document what is present.|The appearance supports interpretation but does not automatically identify an exact object or sequence of events.~Describe before inferring.|One wound feature rarely determines a complete event.~Why should a report separate wound description from suggested cause?~The description is directly observable, while the cause is an interpretation with assumptions and uncertainty.~Describe a fictional diagram using neutral features before offering a qualified interpretation.
asphyxia~Asphyxial mechanisms and oxygen failure~Injuries and environmental effects~4~Cells depend on air entry, gas exchange, blood transport, and circulation for oxygen delivery.|Different circumstances disrupt different steps, so oxygen failure is broader than a blocked airway alone.|Findings such as petechiae are not unique to one cause and must be interpreted with the entire investigation.~Follow the oxygen-delivery chain.|Nonspecific signs cannot establish a specific cause alone.~Why do pinpoint hemorrhages not prove one particular asphyxial mechanism?~They can occur in multiple circumstances, so their presence lacks the specificity needed for that conclusion.~Draw four possible points of failure between inhaled air and tissue oxygen use.
environmental~Drowning, heat, cold and electrical injury~Injuries and environmental effects~4~Environmental injuries depend on the physical exposure, its intensity and duration, and host factors.|Water exposure, temperature extremes, and electricity affect the body through different mechanisms.|Scene information and clinical or postmortem findings need integration; one external mark cannot explain every internal effect.~Exposure type determines the mechanism to investigate.|External appearance can underestimate internal injury.~Why can similar-looking external injuries have different internal consequences?~Energy transfer, route, duration, and tissue properties can differ despite a superficially similar appearance.~Compare exposure, mechanism, and evidence limitations for two environmental injury categories.
sexual-assault~Sexual-assault care, respectful examination and evidence~Professional practice~0~Assessment prioritizes care, consent, privacy, and respectful communication alongside documentation.|Evidence collection and reporting follow current law and trained clinical protocols. Personal assumptions about behavior or appearance are not evidence of consent.|Absence of a particular injury does not by itself establish that an assault did not occur.~Consent to examination is separate from consent to other actions.|Use neutral, nonjudgmental records.~Why must conclusions avoid equating absence of injury with absence of assault?~Injuries depend on many circumstances and may be absent or not visible; the evidential question cannot be settled by that observation alone.~Rewrite a fictional judgmental note as a neutral account of reported and observed facts.
toxicology-basics~Toxic exposure, toxidromes and sample interpretation~Toxicology~5~Toxic effects depend on the substance, amount, route, timing, and the person's condition.|A toxidrome is a pattern suggesting a class of exposures, not definitive identification of one poison.|Laboratory tests vary in what they detect and when; clinical assessment and exposure history remain important.~Detection is not always proof of causation or severity.|A negative test has method and timing limits.~Why can a screening panel miss a real toxic exposure?~It may not include the relevant substance or may be sampled outside a useful detection window.~Build an exposure timeline and list the questions a test would need to answer.
poison-mechanisms~Organophosphates, alcohol and other poison mechanisms~Toxicology~5~Poisons can alter nerve signaling, cellular energy processes, organ function, or oxygen handling through different targets.|Organophosphates inhibit acetylcholinesterase and can produce excessive acetylcholine-related effects. Other substances act through different pathways.|Learning the target helps organize effects, but emergency treatment requires clinical assessment and current protocols.~Name target before memorizing a symptom list.|One mechanism may affect several organs.~Why can inhibiting one signaling enzyme produce effects in several body systems?~The same transmitter system operates at multiple sites, so preventing its normal breakdown changes several functions at once.~Trace enzyme inhibition to prolonged messenger action and multiple target-organ effects.
law-court~Medical law, professional duties and court evidence~Professional practice~0~Legal duties depend on current jurisdiction, facts, and the role of the practitioner.|Medical testimony should explain expertise, observations, reasoning, and limitations without presenting uncertainty as certainty.|Study concepts alongside current Indian legal texts and institutional guidance; historical section numbers may no longer be applicable.~State the basis and limits of an opinion.|Clinical learning notes are not a substitute for current law.~Why should a legal provision be checked by date and jurisdiction?~Rules can change, and similar issues may be treated differently across legal systems or time periods.~Prepare an evidence-based opinion outline with facts, assumptions, reasoning, and limitations.
`;
MBBS_TOPIC_RAW.microbiology = `
structure~Microbial structure, growth and classification~General microbiology~0~Bacteria are cells, viruses require host-cell machinery, and fungi and parasites have other biological features.|Shape, cell wall, genetic material, and growth conditions help organize microbes into useful groups.|Classification is a tool for predicting behavior and choosing tests, not a substitute for identifying an organism in context.~Microbe is a broad category.|Structure helps predict test behavior.~Why is one detection method unlikely to work equally well for every infectious agent?~Agents differ in size, structure, growth needs, and molecules available for detection, so methods reveal different features.~Compare bacteria, viruses, fungi, and parasites by structure and replication needs.
specimens~Specimen collection and diagnostic reasoning~General microbiology~0~A useful test begins with a suitable sample from the relevant site at an appropriate time.|Contamination, poor transport, and prior treatment can change what a laboratory finds.|Culture, microscopy, antigen tests, and genetic tests answer different questions; detection does not always establish causation.~Sample quality limits result quality.|Ask what the test actually detects.~Why might finding bacteria in a sample not prove a bacterial cause of illness?~They may be contaminants or normal colonizers rather than the cause at the suspected disease site.~Create a sample-site-timing-method-interpretation checklist.
microscopy-culture~Stains, culture and susceptibility testing~General microbiology~0~Microscopy reveals visible structures and staining patterns. Gram staining groups many bacteria through wall-related properties.|Culture grows organisms under selected conditions, which can miss agents needing other methods.|Susceptibility testing measures response under standardized conditions and needs clinical interpretation for the infection site.~A stain is not complete species identification.|Laboratory susceptibility and clinical success are related but not identical.~Why can a negative culture coexist with infection?~The agent may not grow under the chosen conditions, the sample may be inadequate, or treatment and timing may reduce recovery.~Compare what microscopy, culture, and susceptibility testing each add.
sterilization~Cleaning, disinfection and sterilization~Infection prevention~5~Cleaning removes material that can interfere with later processing.|Disinfection reduces many harmful microbes, while sterilization aims to eliminate all microbial life including bacterial spores.|The required process depends on equipment use and validated methods, not appearance alone.~Visibly clean is not necessarily sterile.|Spores are an important distinction in process goals.~Why is cleaning still important before a later disinfection or sterilization step?~Remaining material can protect microbes or interfere with the process, making reliable treatment harder.~Arrange cleaning, processing choice, and verification as a conceptual workflow.
infection-control~Transmission, standard precautions and resistance~Infection prevention~5~Infection spreads through routes linking a source with a susceptible host.|Precautions interrupt routes through actions such as hand hygiene, suitable barriers, and safe handling of equipment and samples.|Antimicrobial pressure can select resistant organisms, connecting prevention with responsible drug use.~Find the transmission route before selecting an interruption point.|Resistance is a property of organisms, not the patient's willpower.~Why can preventing infections reduce antimicrobial resistance pressure?~Fewer infections can reduce antimicrobial exposure and opportunities for resistant organisms to spread.~Draw a transmission chain and mark three distinct interruption points.
innate~Innate immunity, barriers and complement~Immunology~1~Skin, mucosal surfaces, and local chemical conditions limit entry of many agents.|Innate immune cells recognize broad danger patterns and can engulf material or coordinate inflammation.|Complement is a group of proteins contributing to recognition, inflammation, and removal through linked pathways.~Innate responses are rapid and pattern-based.|Barriers and cellular responses work together.~Why does a break in skin change infection risk?~It bypasses an important entry barrier, allowing agents access to tissues where further defenses must respond.~Map barrier, recognition, engulfment, and signaling as connected defenses.
adaptive~Antigens, antibodies and cellular immunity~Immunology~1~Adaptive immunity recognizes more specific targets called antigens.|B cells can become antibody-secreting plasma cells; T cells coordinate responses or act on target cells through different subsets.|Memory responses change later encounters, but their effectiveness depends on the agent and immune context.~Antibody and antigen are not the same thing.|B-cell and T-cell functions complement one another.~Why is measuring antibody not a complete description of immunity?~Protection also depends on cellular responses, antibody properties and location, and the infectious agent's biology.~Draw antigen recognition leading to B-cell and T-cell response branches.
vaccines~Vaccines, immune memory and immunodeficiency~Immunology~1~Vaccination presents an antigen or instructions for it in a form designed to prepare immune responses.|Later exposure can then encounter a response shaped by that preparation, although protection is not always complete.|Immunodeficiency can affect different defense components, producing different infection patterns and considerations.~Memory improves preparedness; it is not conscious remembering.|Immune weakness is not one uniform state.~Why can loss of one immune component produce a particular infection pattern?~Different agents are controlled by different combinations of defenses, so a specific missing function creates specific vulnerabilities.~Compare the role of a barrier, antibody, and T-cell response in protection.
cocci~Staphylococci, streptococci and common cocci~Bacteriology~2~Cocci are roughly spherical bacteria, but grouping and other properties distinguish important families.|Staphylococci often form clusters, while streptococci often form chains or pairs. Laboratory and clinical features refine identification.|Disease can result from local invasion, released toxins, or immune consequences, so naming shape is only a first step.~Shape and arrangement organize thinking.|Clinical significance depends on species, site, and context.~Why should a coccal appearance not be treated as a final identification?~Several organisms share that shape; additional tests and the sample context are needed to distinguish them.~Make a morphology-test-disease-mechanism profile for two coccal groups.
enteric~Enteric bacteria and gastrointestinal infection~Bacteriology~2~Gut disease can arise through toxin effects, invasion, or other disruptions of intestinal function.|Transmission often relates to food, water, or contact, but agent biology differs.|The sample and diagnostic approach should match the syndrome and timing rather than treating all diarrhea as one infection.~Watery loss and invasive injury are different mechanisms.|Exposure route helps organize prevention.~Why can two infections both cause diarrhea through different mechanisms?~One may alter fluid secretion while another injures the lining or provokes inflammation; the shared symptom does not prove a shared mechanism.~Compare secretory and inflammatory mechanisms in a simple gut-wall sketch.
respiratory~Respiratory bacteria and mycobacteria~Bacteriology~2~Respiratory pathogens interact with airway defenses and can affect different anatomical levels.|Mycobacteria have lipid-rich walls associated with acid-fast staining properties and particular growth characteristics.|A respiratory sample, imaging, and clinical pattern provide complementary information rather than interchangeable answers.~Site and timing guide sampling.|Acid-fastness is a staining property.~Why can mycobacterial investigation require approaches beyond an ordinary rapid bacterial culture?~The wall properties and growth behavior differ, so special methods and timelines may be needed.~Build an agent-site-sample-test chart for a respiratory infection scenario.
anaerobes-spirochetes~Anaerobes, spirochetes and zoonotic patterns~Bacteriology~2~Some bacteria grow best without oxygen, while others have distinctive structures or transmission patterns.|Anaerobic conditions can arise in particular tissue environments; animal-related exposure can suggest a zoonotic infection.|An exposure clue narrows possibilities but does not replace laboratory or clinical confirmation.~Growth environment and exposure history matter.|A zoonosis is an animal-human infection relationship.~Why can tissue conditions affect which organisms flourish after an injury?~Oxygen availability, blood supply, and damaged material change the local environment and favor different microbial growth needs.~Compare an oxygen-rich surface with a poorly perfused deep tissue space.
viral-basics~Viral replication, latency and diagnostic timing~Virology~3~A virus carries genetic instructions and requires a suitable host cell to make copies.|Entry, replication, assembly, and release involve different molecular steps. Some viruses persist with limited activity and can reactivate.|Genetic material, antigen, and antibody may appear at different times, so tests answer different questions across an infection.~Latency is persistence, not complete disappearance.|Antibody measures a host response, not directly the entire virus.~Why can an antibody test be negative early in an infection?~The immune response may not yet have produced a detectable amount, even if the infectious agent is already present.~Draw an illustrative timeline of agent markers and later antibody response without fixed universal dates.
viral-systems~Respiratory, enteric and nervous-system viruses~Virology~3~Viruses differ in entry route, tissue preference, and patterns of spread.|A respiratory or enteric entry route does not always restrict disease to that organ system.|Interpret a viral syndrome using exposure, timing, tissue involvement, and suitable tests rather than one symptom alone.~Entry site and damage site can differ.|Not all viral infections are short-lived or mild.~Why can a virus entering through one surface cause effects elsewhere?~It may spread within the host and infect other susceptible tissues or provoke systemic responses.~Trace an entry route, possible spread, target tissue, and diagnostic question.
hepatitis-hiv~Viral hepatitis and HIV principles~Virology~3~Hepatitis viruses share a liver-related disease theme but differ in transmission, persistence, and prevention.|HIV affects important immune functions, so infection consequences involve the host's changing defense capacity.|Screening, confirmation, and monitoring serve different purposes and must be interpreted with timing and the specific marker tested.~A shared organ does not mean identical viral biology.|Test purpose matters as much as its name.~Why is a positive viral marker not always enough to describe current disease activity?~Different markers may indicate exposure, immune response, presence of agent, or another stage; their meaning depends on the marker and context.~Compare exposure, diagnosis, and monitoring as three distinct laboratory questions.
fungi~Yeasts, molds and fungal disease~Mycology and parasitology~4~Fungi are distinct from bacteria and may grow as yeasts, molds, or forms that change with conditions.|Some cause superficial disease, while others invade deeper tissues, especially with particular exposure or host factors.|Identification uses sample site, microscopy, growth patterns, and other tests appropriate to the suspected organism.~Superficial and invasive disease differ in tissue depth.|Fungal growth is not bacterial growth.~Why does the same environmental fungus not cause the same disease in every exposed person?~Exposure amount, entry route, and host defenses alter whether it remains controlled or establishes disease.~Draw surface, subcutaneous, and systemic locations and the sample question for each.
protozoa~Protozoa, malaria and vector relationships~Mycology and parasitology~4~Protozoa are single-celled parasites with varied life cycles and tissue targets.|Malaria involves Plasmodium parasites transmitted by mosquitoes, with stages in different host locations.|The agent, vector, host, and stage are separate pieces of the explanation; prevention can act at different points.~Mosquito is the vector, not the malaria parasite.|Stage and burden affect interpretation.~Why does controlling a vector help reduce transmission?~It interrupts movement of the agent between hosts even without directly changing every infection already present.~Draw agent, vector, human host, and life-cycle transitions.
helminths-practicals~Helminths, laboratory recognition and integrated cases~Mycology and parasitology~4~Helminths are parasitic worms whose eggs, larvae, and adults can occupy different sites and hosts.|Diagnostic material depends on where a stage is found, so a single sample type cannot detect every parasite.|Practical study combines specimen recognition with a life-cycle explanation, exposure history, and appropriate interpretation.~Name the stage seen, not just the organism family.|A negative sample has method and timing limits.~Why can stool examination be inappropriate for some tissue-stage parasite questions?~The relevant stage may reside in tissue rather than being released into stool, requiring a different diagnostic approach.~For one teaching parasite, link life-cycle stage, body site, sample, and prevention point.
`;
MBBS_TOPIC_RAW.pharmacology = `
routes~Drug routes, absorption and bioavailability~General pharmacology~0~A medicine's route affects how it enters the body and how rapidly useful concentrations may be reached.|Absorption is movement from the administration site into circulation. Bioavailability is the fraction reaching general circulation unchanged.|Oral drugs may undergo changes before reaching that circulation, so an administered amount is not always the same as systemic exposure.~Dose and exposure are different quantities.|Route changes pharmacokinetics, not just convenience.~Why can the same dose by two routes produce different exposure?~Absorption and processing before general circulation can differ, changing the fraction and timing of drug reaching blood.~Compare oral and intravenous entry using a route-to-circulation diagram.
distribution~Distribution, metabolism, clearance and half-life~General pharmacology~0~After entering blood, drugs distribute between plasma and tissues according to their properties and binding.|Metabolism changes drug molecules; excretion removes drug or its products. Clearance summarizes removal relative to concentration.|Half-life depends on distribution and clearance under the relevant model, so it is not a universal fixed property independent of the patient.~Metabolism is not identical to excretion.|A larger distribution volume can affect half-life.~Why can impaired elimination prolong a drug's effects?~Drug concentrations may decline more slowly, increasing the duration of exposure at relevant targets.~Draw absorption, distribution, metabolism, and excretion as connected compartments.
receptors~Receptors, dose-response, potency and efficacy~General pharmacology~0~A drug acts through interactions with targets such as receptors or enzymes. An agonist activates a receptor; an antagonist blocks activation in the basic model.|Dose-response curves describe how a measured effect changes with dose or concentration.|Potency concerns the amount needed for an effect; efficacy concerns the maximum achievable effect in that setting.~Small dose does not mean larger maximum effect.|Target binding and final response are linked through signaling.~Can a more potent drug have lower efficacy?~Yes. It may produce its effects at lower amounts while reaching a smaller maximum effect than another drug.~Sketch two curves that differ in potency and two that differ in maximum response.
drug-safety~Adverse reactions, interactions and pharmacovigilance~General pharmacology~0~Useful and unwanted effects can arise from the same mechanism or from different targets.|Interactions may change drug concentration or combine physiological effects. Patient conditions influence the resulting risk.|Pharmacovigilance detects and evaluates suspected medicine-related harms; a report is evidence to assess, not automatic proof of causation.~Ask whether an interaction changes exposure or response.|Timing supports assessment but does not prove causation.~Why can two individually familiar medicines create a new problem together?~One may change the other's handling, or their physiological effects may add in a harmful way.~Classify a hypothetical interaction as pharmacokinetic or pharmacodynamic and explain why.
rational-use~Prescription principles and rational medicine use~General pharmacology~0~Rational medicine use starts with a defined problem and treatment goal, followed by evidence about options.|Selection considers expected benefit, harms, patient factors, and monitoring, not simply a remembered brand name.|A prescription is a communication document whose clarity matters; practical prescribing requires current guidance and supervised learning.~Goal → choice → communication → monitoring.|Drug name alone is not a complete plan.~Why should a treatment goal be stated before selecting a drug?~Without a defined goal, the benefit to assess and the evidence needed for selection remain unclear.~Draft the headings of a prescription-review checklist without assigning a patient-specific regimen.
cholinergic~Cholinergic drugs and antimuscarinic drugs~Autonomic pharmacology~1~Acetylcholine acts at muscarinic and nicotinic receptors in different locations.|Drugs can stimulate these pathways, block receptors, or alter acetylcholine breakdown. The target predicts which organs may respond.|A drug acting at one receptor group does not reproduce or block every acetylcholine-related action.~Muscarinic and nicotinic are different receptor groups.|Location explains organ effect.~Why does inhibiting acetylcholinesterase increase acetylcholine-related signaling?~The enzyme normally helps terminate the message by breaking acetylcholine down, so inhibition prolongs its availability at relevant junctions.~Draw release, receptor binding, and breakdown, then mark possible drug targets.
adrenergic~Adrenergic agonists and blockers~Autonomic pharmacology~1~Adrenaline-related signals act through receptor families with different organ effects.|Beta-1 activity supports cardiac function, while beta-2 activity can relax airway smooth muscle. Alpha-receptor actions also vary by subtype and location.|Selectivity is relative, so exposure and tissue context influence the final pattern of effects.~Learn receptor, tissue, and response together.|A selective label does not mean only one possible effect.~Why can activating beta-2 receptors improve airflow?~Relaxation of airway smooth muscle can widen the airway and reduce resistance, although it does not address every possible breathing problem.~Make a receptor-location-effect table for major adrenergic receptor groups.
pressure-heart~Antihypertensives and heart-failure drug principles~Cardiovascular and renal drugs~2~Pressure and circulation can be changed through vessel tone, heart activity, fluid handling, and hormonal pathways.|Drug classes act at different points, so a similar pressure effect does not imply the same mechanism.|In heart failure, the relevant problem includes function and compensatory pathways, not only one blood-pressure number.~Target → physiological change → benefit and harm.|One measured effect does not describe the whole drug.~Why can two drugs both lower pressure while producing different unwanted effects?~They act through different targets and therefore alter other functions differently even when one final measurement changes similarly.~Group cardiovascular drugs by vessel, heart, fluid, or hormonal target.
diuretics~Diuretics and nephron targets~Cardiovascular and renal drugs~2~Different nephron segments reclaim different substances through different transporters.|Diuretics alter this handling, often increasing salt and water excretion. The segment and transporter explain associated electrolyte effects.|Loop diuretics inhibit the sodium-potassium-chloride transporter in the thick ascending limb.~Learn the nephron location before the drug list.|Water and electrolyte effects travel together.~Why does blocking salt reabsorption often increase urine water?~More solute remains in the tubular fluid, influencing water retention within the urine-forming pathway.~Draw a nephron and mark major diuretic target regions.
ischemia-rhythm~Antianginal, antiarrhythmic and lipid-lowering principles~Cardiovascular and renal drugs~2~Myocardial oxygen balance depends on supply and demand, while rhythm depends on electrical activation and conduction.|Drug groups can reduce demand, alter vessels, change electrical properties, or modify lipid-related risk pathways.|These are separate objectives; symptom relief, rhythm control, and long-term risk reduction should not be treated as synonyms.~Name the objective of each class.|Electrical effects can influence rhythm and conduction together.~Why does relief of chest discomfort not by itself demonstrate reversal of all coronary disease?~Reducing a demand-supply imbalance can improve symptoms without removing every underlying structural or risk-related problem.~Create separate columns for symptom, rhythm, and risk-modifying mechanisms.
antithrombotics~Antiplatelets, anticoagulants and clot breakdown~Cardiovascular and renal drugs~2~Platelets form part of the initial injury response, while clotting proteins build stabilizing fibrin.|Antiplatelets and anticoagulants target different components. Drugs promoting clot breakdown act on another stage.|Benefit and bleeding risk both arise from altering normal hemostatic mechanisms.~Preventing growth and breaking down a clot are different actions.|Not all antithrombotic drugs are interchangeable.~Why can interfering with clot formation increase bleeding risk?~The same hemostatic pathways help prevent harmful clots and limit normal bleeding after injury.~Draw a clot pathway and locate platelet, coagulation, and breakdown targets.
cns~Sedatives, antiseizure and psychiatric drug principles~CNS and pain~3~Brain function depends on balanced communication between nerve cells.|Drugs alter receptors, transmitter availability, or ion channels to change patterns of signaling. Effects depend on circuits and targets.|A sedating effect is not equivalent to treating every cause of agitation, insomnia, or seizure-like activity.~A symptom and its cause are different treatment questions.|CNS drugs have distinct mechanisms and objectives.~Why can changing an inhibitory signaling pathway affect both intended symptoms and alertness?~The pathway may participate in several brain functions, so target effects need not be restricted to one desired outcome.~Compare receptor, transmitter-handling, and ion-channel mechanisms.
analgesics~NSAIDs, opioids and pain mechanisms~CNS and pain~3~Pain involves tissue signals, nerve transmission, and brain processing. Different drugs act at different points.|NSAIDs reduce cyclooxygenase-dependent prostaglandin production, while opioids act through opioid receptors.|Because these pathways also serve other functions, useful pain relief can accompany unwanted effects.~Analgesia is not the same as anesthesia.|Mechanism helps predict both benefit and harm.~Why does reducing prostaglandins have effects beyond pain?~Prostaglandins participate in several normal physiological processes, so their reduction is not restricted to pain signaling.~Map peripheral tissue signaling, nerve transmission, and central processing with possible drug targets.
anesthetic~Local anesthetics and general-anesthesia components~CNS and pain~3~Local anesthetics interrupt nerve signaling by blocking voltage-gated sodium channels.|General anesthesia combines effects such as unconsciousness, analgesia, and immobility through different agents or approaches.|Neuromuscular blockade prevents skeletal-muscle activation but does not itself provide unconsciousness or pain relief.~Movement, awareness, and pain are separate dimensions.|Blocking conduction differs from suppressing consciousness.~Why is immobility insufficient evidence of adequate anesthesia?~A muscle blocker can prevent movement while awareness and pain pathways remain active, so separate assessment and treatment components are required.~Make a three-column chart for awareness, pain, and movement effects.
antibacterials~Antibacterial targets and resistance~Anti-infective and anticancer drugs~4~Antibacterials exploit differences in bacterial cell walls, protein synthesis, genetic processes, or metabolism.|Resistance can arise through altered targets, drug destruction, reduced entry, or increased drug removal.|Selecting an agent requires organism, site, susceptibility, and patient context; a mechanism alone does not determine a complete regimen.~Target and resistance mechanism should be paired.|Antibiotics do not routinely treat viral infections.~Why can an antibiotic fail even when its target exists in the bacterium?~The drug may be destroyed, kept out, or pumped out, preventing adequate action at the target.~For one antibacterial target, draw two possible resistance routes.
other-antimicrobials~Antiviral, antifungal and antiparasitic principles~Anti-infective and anticancer drugs~4~Viruses, fungi, and parasites have different biology, so useful drug targets differ.|Viruses use host machinery, fungi have distinctive membranes and walls, and parasites may change forms across a life cycle.|The stage and location of an organism influence which intervention can act effectively.~Identify the agent group and life-cycle stage.|One antimicrobial category cannot replace another.~Why can a drug effective against one parasite stage fail against another?~Different stages can occupy different tissues or express different targets, altering exposure and susceptibility.~Draw a life cycle and mark how target presence might change between stages.
endocrine~Diabetes, thyroid, steroid and reproductive drugs~Endocrine drugs~5~Endocrine drugs may replace a missing signal, reduce excessive hormone action, or change production and feedback.|Glucose-lowering classes affect different processes, including insulin supply, tissue response, or nutrient and glucose handling.|A hormone's many target tissues explain why useful and unwanted effects can occur in several organs.~Replacement and suppression are different goals.|Assess the whole axis, not a single hormone label.~Why does replacing insulin differ from increasing a tissue's response to insulin?~One changes signal availability while the other changes how effectively that signal works; the physiological starting problems differ.~Classify endocrine interventions as replacement, synthesis change, or target-response change.
gi-respiratory-cancer~GI, respiratory and anticancer mechanisms~System-based drug integration~5~Proton-pump inhibitors reduce stomach acid secretion; bronchodilators relax airway muscle, and inhaled corticosteroids reduce inflammation.|Cancer drugs may affect cell division, signaling, or immune interactions. Their targets also explain many effects on normal tissues.|Organ-based grouping is a starting organizer; mechanism remains the connection needed for reasoning.~Same organ does not mean same target.|A useful response requires monitoring alongside harms.~Why can a bronchodilator and an anti-inflammatory airway drug serve different roles?~One changes airway muscle tone while the other addresses inflammatory processes, so they act on different contributors to airway narrowing.~Build a target-effect-adverse-effect table for one GI, one respiratory, and one anticancer class.
`;
MBBS_TOPIC_RAW.pathology = `
cell-adaptation~Cell adaptation, injury and death~General pathology~0~Cells can respond to demand by changing size, number, or mature type. Hypertrophy, hyperplasia, and metaplasia name these different adaptations.|Injury occurs when stress exceeds the ability to maintain function. Its reversibility depends on the type, severity, and duration of the insult.|Necrosis and apoptosis describe different patterns of cell death and surrounding response.~Bigger cells and more cells are different mechanisms.|Adaptation can precede injury but is not always disease.~Why can a larger organ result from different cellular processes?~Its cells may be larger, more numerous, or both. The organ's size alone does not distinguish hypertrophy from hyperplasia.~Make a stress-adaptation-injury-outcome flow chart.
inflammation~Acute and chronic inflammation~General pathology~1~Inflammation is a coordinated response involving signals, vessels, and immune cells. It can follow infection or noninfectious injury.|Acute patterns often involve rapid vessel changes and neutrophils; chronic patterns involve persistent injury, immune activity, and repair together.|The appearance must be interpreted with timing and cause, rather than assuming every inflammatory focus contains microbes.~Inflammation does not equal infection.|Chronic inflammation can include damage and repair at the same time.~Why can inflammation continue after an initial injury?~A persistent trigger, ongoing immune response, or continuing tissue damage may maintain it rather than allowing resolution.~Compare acute and chronic inflammation by timing, cells, and tissue outcome.
repair~Regeneration, wound healing and fibrosis~General pathology~1~Repair can replace lost cells or fill a defect with supporting scar tissue.|Regeneration depends on remaining cells, tissue capacity, and the supporting framework. Scarring restores continuity but may not restore the original function fully.|Blood supply, infection, nutrition, and mechanical conditions influence healing.~Closure and full functional restoration are different outcomes.|Fibrosis can help seal damage but also impair organ function.~Why can a healed wound still have reduced flexibility?~Scar tissue may replace the original arrangement of cells and matrix, restoring continuity without reproducing the same mechanical properties.~Draw injury, inflammation, rebuilding, and remodeling as overlapping stages.
edema-shock~Edema, congestion and shock~Vascular and immune injury~2~Fluid movement between vessels and tissues depends on pressure forces, barrier properties, and lymph drainage.|Edema is excess tissue fluid; congestion is excess blood related to impaired outflow. They can coexist but are not synonyms.|Shock describes inadequate effective tissue perfusion, with different causes involving volume, pumping, distribution, or obstruction.~Fluid location matters.|A swollen tissue is not necessarily receiving adequate blood flow.~Why can edema occur when lymph drainage is blocked?~Fluid normally returned through lymph routes accumulates when that clearance pathway is impaired.~Map fluid leaving capillaries and returning through blood and lymph routes.
thrombosis~Thrombosis, embolism and infarction~Vascular and immune injury~2~Clots can form inside vessels when lining injury, abnormal flow, or increased clotting tendency favors them.|A piece may travel as an embolus and obstruct another vessel. Infarction is tissue death from inadequate blood supply.|The outcome depends on the blocked vessel, alternate supply, tissue demand, and duration.~Thrombus forms; embolus travels; infarction is a tissue outcome.|Not every embolus is a blood clot.~Why does a clot not always produce an infarct?~Its effect depends on how much flow is lost and whether another supply can meet the tissue's needs.~Draw a clot at its origin, a traveling fragment, and a possible downstream effect.
immune-injury~Hypersensitivity and autoimmune injury~Vascular and immune injury~2~Immune responses can damage tissue while responding to an external target or the body's own components.|Hypersensitivity categories group mechanisms such as antibody-related effects, immune complexes, and T-cell-mediated injury.|Autoimmune disease involves loss of appropriate tolerance to self components; the affected target helps determine the disease pattern.~Types I–IV are mechanisms, not severity grades.|Type IV is mainly T-cell mediated.~Why can similar symptoms arise from different immune mechanisms?~Different pathways can produce overlapping tissue inflammation or damage, so the appearance alone may not identify the initiating mechanism.~Compare antibody-mediated and T-cell-mediated injury in a two-column mechanism chart.
neoplasia~Tumor growth, invasion and spread~Neoplasia~3~Abnormal growth develops through changes affecting growth control, survival, and other cellular functions.|Malignant cells invade surrounding tissue and may spread to distant sites. Benign tumors do not metastasize but can still cause local or hormonal problems.|A tumor's effect depends on its biology, location, size, and relationship to normal structures.~Benign does not mean harmless in every location.|Invasion and distant spread are related but distinct.~Why can a benign intracranial mass still be serious?~A confined space and proximity to vital structures can make pressure or compression harmful even without metastatic behavior.~Map growth, local effects, invasion, and distant spread as separate possibilities.
grade-stage~Tumor genes, grade and stage~Neoplasia~3~Growth-promoting genes and growth-restraining genes contribute differently to normal cell control.|Tumor grade describes features such as differentiation and cellular appearance. Stage describes the extent of disease in the body.|Both matter, but one does not substitute for the other; a microscopic appearance cannot alone map anatomical spread.~Grade asks about tumor features.|Stage asks how far disease extends.~Can two tumors with the same grade have different stages?~Yes. Similar cellular appearance can occur with different anatomical extent, because grade and stage measure different dimensions.~Create two hypothetical tumors with the same grade but different spread and explain the distinction.
anemia~Anemia, iron deficiency and megaloblastic patterns~Hematopathology~4~Anemia means hemoglobin below the relevant reference range. Consider reduced production, blood loss, and increased destruction.|Cell size and reticulocyte response organize the investigation: small cells often suggest impaired hemoglobin formation, while other patterns point elsewhere.|Iron deficiency and B12 or folate deficiency affect different biochemical processes, so their morphology and associated findings differ.~Anemia is a description requiring a cause.|Cell size is a clue, not a complete diagnosis.~Why is the reticulocyte response useful in anemia?~It provides information about marrow response and helps distinguish insufficient production from an appropriate response to loss or destruction.~Build an anemia tree using mechanism, cell size, and marrow response.
hemolysis~Hemolysis and hemoglobin disorders~Hematopathology~4~Hemolysis is premature red-cell destruction. It can arise from defects within red cells or external stresses.|Hemoglobin disorders alter hemoglobin structure or production, changing red-cell survival and oxygen-related function in different ways.|The marrow may compensate by increasing production, so the observed anemia reflects both destruction and replacement.~Destruction rate and production response both matter.|A structural hemoglobin change differs from reduced chain production.~Why may increased red-cell destruction not immediately cause severe anemia?~Compensatory production may partly offset the loss, depending on marrow capacity and nutrient availability.~Draw red-cell lifespan, destruction, and compensatory production as a balance.
leukemia-lymphoma~Leukemia, lymphoma and lymph-node patterns~Hematopathology~4~White-cell and lymphoid disorders include reactive changes as well as clonal malignancies.|Leukemia often involves marrow and blood; lymphoma typically forms lymphoid tissue masses, although overlap occurs.|Classification depends on cell lineage, maturation, and laboratory or tissue features rather than white-cell count alone.~Reactive increase is not automatically cancer.|Lineage and maturation help classify abnormal cells.~Why does a high white-cell count not establish leukemia?~Infection, inflammation, and other reactive processes can also raise the count. Cell features and the wider clinical picture are needed.~Compare a reactive lymph-node process with a clonal process by the questions each requires.
bleeding~Platelet disorders and coagulation defects~Hematopathology~4~Platelets and coagulation proteins contribute different parts of hemostasis.|Platelet problems often produce skin or mucosal bleeding patterns, while some factor defects cause deeper bleeding; these are tendencies, not absolute rules.|Laboratory tests examine selected components and must be interpreted with the bleeding history.~Count, function, and coagulation-factor activity are different questions.|Pattern supports reasoning but is not definitive alone.~Why can normal coagulation screening coexist with a platelet-related bleeding problem?~Screening tests of clotting pathways do not directly measure every aspect of platelet number or function.~Make a platelet-versus-factor comparison with mechanism and typical pattern.
cardiovascular~Atherosclerosis, ischemic injury and heart pathology~Systemic pathology~5~Atherosclerosis involves lipid accumulation and inflammation in arterial walls, producing plaques that can narrow flow or undergo complications.|Heart injury may affect muscle, valves, lining, or vessels; each location changes function differently.|Ischemic damage depends on the balance between supply and demand and the duration of inadequate supply.~An arterial plaque and a blood clot are different structures.|Structure must be linked to the resulting functional change.~Why can sudden plaque-related thrombosis be more disruptive than a stable narrowing?~A rapid reduction in flow may leave little time for alternative supply or adaptation and can sharply worsen tissue oxygen balance.~Trace arterial-wall change to flow disturbance and myocardial consequence.
respiratory~Pneumonia, obstructive lung disease and lung tumors~Systemic pathology~5~Lung pathology can affect airways, air spaces, supporting tissue, or vessels.|Airway narrowing limits flow, while fluid or cells in air spaces can impair gas exchange. Destruction of exchange surfaces creates another mechanism.|A chest image or microscopic pattern needs a cause and a functional explanation, not only a descriptive label.~Locate the lesion before explaining breathlessness.|Several mechanisms can impair oxygenation.~Why can pneumonia affect oxygen transfer even if major airways are open?~Inflammatory material in exchange regions can interfere with ventilation and diffusion relative to their blood supply.~Draw four lung compartments and one possible injury mechanism in each.
renal~Glomerular, tubular and chronic kidney disease~Systemic pathology~5~Kidney disease can begin in filters, tubes, supporting tissue, or vessels.|Nephritic patterns emphasize filter inflammation and blood in urine, while nephrotic patterns emphasize substantial protein leakage.|Persistent injury can reduce functioning tissue and lead to chronic loss of filtration and regulatory capacity.~Nephritic and nephrotic are patterns, not single causes.|Filtration loss and protein leakage are different abnormalities.~Why can a kidney disorder cause swelling?~Protein loss, salt and water retention, or several combined mechanisms can shift fluid into tissues. The exact route depends on the disorder.~Compare nephritic and nephrotic patterns by dominant abnormality and consequence.
gi-liver~Gastrointestinal inflammation, liver injury and cirrhosis~Systemic pathology~5~The gut requires an intact barrier, coordinated movement, and absorption; disease can disturb any of these.|The liver processes substances and carries blood through an organized structure. Repeated injury may produce fibrosis and regenerative nodules.|Cirrhosis changes both cellular function and blood-flow architecture, so its effects extend beyond a scar's appearance.~Cirrhosis is an architectural change with functional consequences.|Gut symptoms can arise through different tissue mechanisms.~Why can liver scarring alter blood flow?~Fibrous tissue and nodular restructuring distort the normal vascular routes, increasing resistance and changing passage through the organ.~Draw normal liver flow beside a simplified scarred architecture.
endocrine-reproductive~Endocrine, breast and reproductive pathology~Systemic pathology~3~Hormone-producing tissues can cause disease through excess output, deficient output, or a mass effect.|Breast and reproductive lesions include inflammatory, benign, and malignant processes. Tissue of origin helps organize them.|A lump's behavior cannot reliably be determined from size or feel alone; structural and functional assessment answer different questions.~Hormone activity and tumor behavior are separate dimensions.|Tissue diagnosis and anatomical extent are different assessments.~Why can a small endocrine lesion have widespread effects?~A small amount of abnormal hormone-producing tissue may change signals reaching many distant target organs.~Create a mass-effect versus hormone-effect comparison for an endocrine lesion.
practical-pathology~Specimens, microscopy and pathology reports~Practical study~0~A pathology report connects a sample, its preparation, and observed features with an interpretation.|Sampling determines what tissue is available; a small sample may not represent every part of a lesion.|Describe visible findings first, then explain what they suggest and what remains uncertain. Practical recognition requires supervised specimen and slide study.~Observation comes before interpretation.|A sample is part of a larger biological context.~Why can two samples from one lesion yield different information?~Different regions may contain different cells or changes, and preparation or sampling adequacy can affect what is visible.~Write a practice report with specimen, description, interpretation, and limitation headings.
`;
MBBS_TOPIC_RAW.anatomy = `
position~Anatomical position, directions and planes~General anatomy~0~Anatomical position is the shared starting map: upright, looking forward, arms by the sides, palms forward.|Medial means toward the middle line; lateral means away from it. Proximal and distal describe distance from an attachment or origin.|Planes are imaginary cuts: sagittal separates left and right portions, coronal front and back, and transverse upper and lower portions.~Directions refer to the patient, not the viewer.|A plane describes a section, not an organ.~Why can a patient's right hand appear on the left of a front-view diagram?~The diagram faces you. Anatomical directions still refer to the patient's own body, so the observer's view does not change right and left.~Draw a standing figure and label six direction terms with arrows.
tissues~Epithelium and connective tissues~General anatomy~0~Epithelium covers surfaces and forms many glands. Its closely arranged cells help protect, absorb, or secrete.|Connective tissue contains cells within supporting material called extracellular matrix. Its composition explains why bone is rigid while loose connective tissue is flexible.|Identify a tissue by its organization and job, then connect it to an organ that needs that job.~Tissue identity involves cells and their arrangement.|One organ contains several tissue types.~Why does the intestine need both epithelium and connective tissue?~The lining controls exchange with gut contents, while supporting tissue carries vessels and gives structural support. One tissue cannot perform all these roles alone.~Compare a lining and supporting tissue in a two-column sketch.
bones-joints~Bones, cartilage and joints~General anatomy~0~Bone is living tissue containing a mineral-reinforced framework. It supports the body and is continuously renewed.|Cartilage is a flexible supporting tissue; at many joints it provides a smooth surface. Joints connect bones and allow different degrees of movement.|Joint movement depends on surface shape, supporting ligaments, and muscle forces, so anatomy predicts function.~Bone is living, not an inert stick.|A tendon usually connects muscle to bone; a ligament supports bone-to-bone connections.~Why does knowing a joint's shape help predict movement?~The contacting surfaces guide which directions are possible, while ligaments and muscles limit or produce movement. Shape is part of the movement system.~Compare a hinge-type joint with a ball-and-socket joint using labeled arrows.
skin-fascia~Skin, fascia and neurovascular bundles~General anatomy~0~Skin forms a barrier and contains sensory structures. Under it, connective tissues support vessels, nerves, and muscles.|Fascia is connective tissue that surrounds or separates structures. It can organize muscles into compartments.|A neurovascular bundle contains nerves and blood vessels traveling together; proximity helps explain why one injury may affect both sensation and circulation.~Follow layers from surface to depth.|Nearby structures may share an injury mechanism.~Why should a limb injury assessment include both movement and blood supply?~Nerves, muscles, and vessels lie close together. Damage to one region can impair nerve signaling as well as blood flow.~Draw a skin-to-muscle cross-section with a nerve and vessel.
pectoral-axilla~Pectoral region, breast and axilla~Upper limb~1~The pectoral region forms the front of the upper chest; the axilla is the armpit space between chest and upper limb.|Major vessels and brachial plexus branches travel through the axilla. Lymph nodes there receive lymph from several neighboring areas, including much of the breast.|Study boundaries and contents together: a space matters because of what passes through it.~A boundary is different from a content.|Lymph drainage is a route, not proof of disease spread.~Why can an axillary problem affect an upper limb?~The axilla is a passage for important nerves and vessels supplying the limb, so a mass or injury there can disturb their function.~Sketch the axilla as a space and list its main content categories.
brachial-plexus~Brachial plexus and major arm nerves~Upper limb~1~The brachial plexus reorganizes nerve fibers from spinal levels, usually C5 to T1, into nerves supplying the arm.|The radial nerve supports many extension movements; median and ulnar nerves have different forearm and hand territories.|A lesion is localized by combining movement loss, sensory change, and the anatomical route rather than memorizing one symptom alone.~Root → network → named nerve → target.|Motor and sensory findings should fit the same location.~Why is wrist drop a clue rather than a complete diagnosis?~It suggests weakness of wrist extension, often involving the radial pathway, but the level and cause require other movement and sensory findings.~Trace one route from a spinal root through the plexus to a hand function.
arm-hand~Arm, forearm and hand compartments~Upper limb~1~Muscles are organized into groups with related actions and nerve supplies. Forearm groups mainly bend or straighten the wrist and fingers.|Small hand muscles make precise movements such as finger spreading and thumb positioning. Tendons transfer force from muscles to their attachments.|Study an action by identifying the joint crossed, line of pull, and nerve supply.~Muscles pull through tendons.|A movement may involve more than one muscle.~Why can a forearm muscle move a finger?~Its tendon crosses the relevant joints and attaches distally, so contraction transfers force to the finger even though the muscle belly is in the forearm.~Draw a finger tendon route and label the joints it crosses.
gluteal-thigh~Gluteal region, hip and thigh~Lower limb~2~Hip muscles move the thigh and stabilize the pelvis. Stabilization becomes especially important when only one foot supports body weight.|The quadriceps at the front of the thigh straighten the knee and receive femoral nerve supply.|The sciatic nerve passes through the back of the thigh and continues into branches serving much of the leg and foot.~Hip stability and knee movement are different jobs.|Quadriceps extend the knee.~Why is standing on one leg an active task?~Hip stabilizing muscles must resist the tendency of the unsupported side of the pelvis to drop. Stillness can require continuous muscle force.~Draw a pelvis supported on one leg and show the stabilizing muscle pull.
leg-foot~Leg, ankle and foot arches~Lower limb~2~The tibia and fibula support muscle attachments and help form the ankle region. Leg compartments contain muscles with related movements.|Tibial and common fibular nerve branches supply different functional groups. Loss of the ability to lift the front of the foot produces foot drop.|Foot arches distribute load and depend on bone shape, ligaments, and muscular support.~Dorsiflexion lifts the front of the foot.|An arch is a supported arrangement, not empty space alone.~Why can foot drop change walking even when knee movement is preserved?~The toes may not clear the ground during the swing phase, so the person must compensate at other joints.~Sketch ankle dorsiflexion and plantarflexion, then label the nerve groups to investigate.
thorax~Thoracic wall, pleura and lungs~Thorax~3~Ribs, muscles, and the diaphragm change chest volume during breathing. The lungs expand with the chest through the pleural relationship.|Pleura are thin coverings around the lungs and inner chest wall, with a potential space between them.|Airways conduct air, while alveoli provide exchange surfaces; location and function must be learned together.~Pleural space and airway space are different.|The diaphragm separates chest and abdomen.~Why does damage to the pleural relationship affect lung expansion?~Normal mechanical coupling helps the lung follow chest expansion. Air or fluid in the pleural space can disturb that relationship.~Draw a lung, its two pleural layers, and the diaphragm.
heart-mediastinum~Heart, coronary vessels and mediastinum~Thorax~3~The mediastinum is the central chest region between the lungs and contains the heart and other structures.|The heart has four chambers; valves help maintain forward flow. Coronary arteries supply the heart muscle itself.|Knowing blood inside a chamber is different from blood supplying its wall prevents confusion about heart nutrition.~Right heart → lungs; left heart → body.|Coronary supply feeds the heart muscle.~Why can heart muscle become ischemic while its chambers contain blood?~The muscle depends on its own coronary circulation for delivery through tissue vessels. Blood within a chamber does not replace that supply.~Draw the chamber flow route and a separate coronary-supply arrow.
abdominal-wall~Abdominal wall, inguinal canal and peritoneum~Abdomen and pelvis~3~Abdominal-wall muscles support contents and contribute to pressure changes. Openings and passages create anatomically important weak regions.|The inguinal canal is a passage through the lower abdominal wall. Hernias occur when tissue protrudes through an opening or weakness.|The peritoneum lines the abdominal cavity and covers many organs; folds carry vessels and support relations.~Wall, cavity and organ covering are different layers.|A hernia is protrusion, not simply swelling.~Why are normal openings relevant to hernias?~A necessary passage interrupts the otherwise continuous wall and may provide a route through which tissue protrudes when supporting conditions permit.~Sketch the abdominal wall in layers and mark an inguinal passage.
digestive-viscera~Digestive organs, portal circulation and liver~Abdomen and pelvis~3~The digestive tube processes and absorbs food; its regions have different structures suited to their tasks.|Much absorbed material enters blood that travels through the portal vein to the liver. The liver processes nutrients before that blood returns to the general circulation.|Bile ducts carry bile, while portal vessels carry blood. Distinguishing tube contents is essential.~Food stays in the gut lumen until digested and absorbed.|Portal vein carries blood, not food pieces.~Why does the liver receive blood from the intestine?~The portal route delivers absorbed substances for processing and regulation before wider distribution.~Trace a nutrient from the gut lumen to blood, portal vein, and liver.
pelvis-perineum~Urinary organs, pelvis and perineum~Abdomen and pelvis~3~Kidneys produce urine, ureters carry it to the bladder, and the urethra carries it out. Storage and production occur in different organs.|The pelvis contains closely related urinary, reproductive, and lower digestive structures; the perineum lies below the pelvic floor.|Neighboring vessels and tubes must be distinguished by route, not just by name.~Ureter: kidney to bladder; urethra: bladder to outside.|Pelvic relationships explain shared symptoms and procedural risk.~Why can confusing ureter and urethra disrupt a urinary-system explanation?~They are different tubes at different stages: one transports urine into storage, the other carries it out of the body.~Draw the full urine route and label the pelvic organs beside it.
head-neck~Head and neck spaces, glands and cranial nerves~Head and nervous system~4~The head and neck contain tightly packed routes for air, food, blood, and nerve signals.|Cranial nerves have particular sensory, movement, or automatic-control roles. Glands such as thyroid and salivary glands have distinct locations and outputs.|Learn each region by boundaries, contents, and neighboring structures before memorizing isolated lists.~Air and food share part of the pharyngeal route.|A nerve's function helps interpret a local lesion.~Why can a neck mass affect swallowing, voice, or breathing differently?~Its position determines which nearby passage, muscle, or nerve is affected. The same size in a different location can produce a different effect.~Make a route map for air, food, and major nerve functions in the neck.
brain-spinal~Brain, spinal cord, tracts and CSF~Head and nervous system~4~The brain and spinal cord process information and send commands along organized pathways called tracts.|Many corticospinal movement fibers cross in the lower medulla. Pathway crossing helps explain opposite-sided effects of some brain lesions.|Cerebrospinal fluid circulates through brain spaces and around the central nervous system; a blockage can alter upstream fluid spaces.~Mark crossings when drawing a pathway.|Fluid spaces and nerve pathways are different maps.~Why does the side of a lesion not always match the side of weakness?~Some pathways cross, so the relationship depends on whether the lesion lies before or after the crossing.~Draw a descending movement pathway with its crossing and target side.
embryology~Early development, placenta and organ formation~Development and microscopic anatomy~5~Fertilization begins a new developmental sequence of cell division, movement, and specialization. Timing matters because organs form through ordered steps.|Three early layers, ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm, contribute to different tissues. Folding and growth change where those tissues lie.|The placenta supports exchange during pregnancy; it is an interface between circulations rather than a simple mixing tank.~Development is a sequence, not a miniature adult growing larger.|Congenital does not always mean inherited.~Why can an early developmental disturbance affect more than one organ?~Organs may share an early cell origin, signaling event, or developmental period, so a disturbance can affect several related structures.~Create a three-layer chart with two tissue examples per layer.
histology-genetics~Organ histology, chromosomes and inheritance~Development and microscopic anatomy~5~Histology identifies tissue organization under a microscope. Start with the overall pattern, then identify cell types and supporting structures.|Chromosomes organize DNA, and genes contain information used in cell function. Inheritance patterns describe transmission but do not alone predict every feature.|Connect a microscopic feature to a function, such as a thin exchange surface or a secretory cell arrangement.~Pattern → cell → organ function.|A genetic variant's effect depends on its biological context.~Why should a histology answer include function as well as a label?~The arrangement is meaningful because it supports a task. Explaining that link demonstrates understanding beyond recognizing an image.~Choose a teaching slide and explain two visible features in terms of function.
`;
MBBS_TOPIC_RAW.physiology = `
feedback~Homeostasis and feedback control~General physiology~0~Homeostasis keeps internal conditions within workable ranges through continual adjustment.|A sensor detects a variable, a control system compares or integrates information, and effectors change the variable.|Negative feedback opposes a disturbance. Positive feedback reinforces a change until a stopping event ends the loop.~Identify the variable before naming the feedback.|Negative does not mean harmful.~Why is sweating during heat exposure an example of negative feedback?~Evaporation can remove heat, reducing the temperature disturbance that helped trigger the response.~Draw a temperature-control loop with sensor, response, and feedback arrow.
membrane-transport~Diffusion, osmosis and membrane transport~General physiology~0~Diffusion causes net movement down a concentration difference. Osmosis is water movement across a selectively permeable boundary.|Transport proteins create pathways for substances that cannot cross the lipid membrane freely. Active transport uses energy directly or indirectly.|Membrane permeability and the direction of a gradient together determine what can move and which way net movement occurs.~Diffusion is not the same as active pumping.|Osmosis describes water, not all solutes.~Why can two substances move differently across the same membrane?~They may have different gradients and different available channels or carriers. A membrane is selective rather than equally open to everything.~Compare diffusion, facilitated transport, and active transport in a small table.
electrical-signals~Resting potential and action potentials~Nerve and muscle~1~Unequal ion distributions and selective membrane permeability create a voltage difference across a cell membrane.|An action potential is a brief, self-propagating electrical change involving ion channels. In a single excitable fiber it follows an all-or-none principle.|Information can be carried by timing and frequency of impulses, rather than simply making each impulse larger.~Voltage is a difference across a membrane.|All-or-none applies to a single action potential, not all whole-organ responses.~How can a stronger stimulus produce a stronger message if each action potential is all-or-none?~It can change impulse frequency and recruit additional fibers. Signal strength need not depend on making each individual action potential larger.~Sketch voltage against time and label resting state, rise, and recovery.
muscle~Neuromuscular transmission and muscle contraction~Nerve and muscle~1~A motor nerve releases acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction. Receptor activation triggers an electrical response in the muscle.|This leads to calcium release inside the muscle fiber. Calcium allows actin and myosin interactions that produce force.|ATP supports cross-bridge cycling and restoration processes; skeletal and smooth muscle regulate contraction differently.~Nerve message → calcium → filament interaction.|Filaments slide; they do not shrink into shorter molecules.~Why can blocking nerve-muscle communication stop movement even when muscle proteins are intact?~The contractile machinery needs the initiating signal and calcium change. Without that trigger, an intact structure may remain inactive.~Draw four labeled stages from motor nerve to muscle shortening.
blood-cells~Blood composition and red-cell production~Blood physiology~2~Plasma is the liquid component of blood; cells and cell fragments carry out specialized jobs.|Red cells carry hemoglobin, white cells participate in defense, and platelets help hemostasis. The bone marrow produces blood cells through regulated development.|Oxygen-related signals influence red-cell production, linking transport capacity with tissue needs.~Hemoglobin and red-cell count are related but different measures.|Production, loss, and destruction all affect cell numbers.~Why might a low red-cell level have more than one cause?~Cells may be produced too slowly, lost through bleeding, or destroyed too quickly. The final count alone does not distinguish these mechanisms.~Make a production-loss-destruction diagram for red cells.
hemostasis~Platelets, coagulation and blood groups~Blood physiology~2~Vessel injury activates local mechanisms that limit bleeding. Platelets form an initial plug, and clotting proteins help reinforce it with fibrin.|Regulatory and clot-removal systems prevent clotting from spreading unnecessarily and help restore flow.|Blood groups concern antigens on red cells and compatible immune interactions, so transfusion requires more than matching appearance.~Platelet plug and coagulation are linked but different.|Clot formation must be localized and regulated.~Why can bleeding occur despite a normal platelet count?~Platelet function or clotting-protein activity may be abnormal even if the number of platelets is within range.~Draw the sequence from vessel injury to plug, reinforcement, and resolution.
cardiac-cycle~Cardiac cycle and heart sounds~Cardiovascular physiology~2~The heart alternates filling and ejection. Pressure differences determine when valves open or close.|Systole refers to contraction, and diastole to relaxation and filling in the relevant chamber.|Heart sounds relate mainly to vibrations associated with valve closure and surrounding structures; relate them to the cycle instead of memorizing isolated noises.~Flow follows pressure differences.|Valve movement is linked to pressure, not a separate motor.~Why does a valve close when pressure reverses across it?~Its structure permits forward flow but resists reverse flow, so a reversed gradient pushes it toward closure.~Draw a simplified filling-ejection timeline and mark valve changes.
ecg~Cardiac conduction and the ECG~Cardiovascular physiology~2~Specialized cardiac cells initiate and conduct electrical activity in an ordered sequence.|An ECG records voltage changes detected at the body surface as cardiac activation changes over time.|Electrical events normally precede mechanical responses, but an ECG does not directly measure the amount of blood pumped.~Electrical activation is not identical to effective contraction.|Interpret rate and rhythm in a consistent order.~Why can an ECG not directly tell you the stroke volume?~It records electrical activity rather than ventricular filling and ejected volume. Mechanical function requires other observations.~Place atrial and ventricular electrical events before their corresponding mechanical events on a timeline.
pressure-flow~Cardiac output, resistance and blood pressure~Cardiovascular physiology~2~Cardiac output equals heart rate multiplied by stroke volume. It measures volume delivered per minute.|Flow through vessels depends on pressure differences and resistance. Narrower vessels can increase resistance markedly.|Blood pressure regulation includes rapid nerve responses and longer-term changes in fluid and hormonal control.~Keep units beside every variable.|Pressure is not the same quantity as flow.~What output results from 60 beats/min and 80 mL/beat?~The product is 4,800 mL/min, or 4.8 L/min. The beats unit cancels, leaving volume per time.~Calculate two outputs with different rates and stroke volumes and compare the results.
breathing-mechanics~Mechanics of breathing and lung volumes~Respiratory physiology~3~Breathing muscles change chest volume, creating pressure differences that move air.|Airway resistance affects flow; lung and chest-wall compliance affect how easily volume changes.|Lung volumes describe amounts of air, while flow measures how quickly air moves. A complete interpretation may need both.~Compliance concerns expansion per pressure change.|Volume and flow are different measurements.~Why can a person have difficulty moving air despite generating breathing effort?~High airway resistance or reduced ability of the chest-lung system to expand can limit the resulting airflow or volume change.~Draw inspiration as muscle action, volume change, pressure change, and airflow.
gas-exchange~Gas exchange, hemoglobin and oxygen delivery~Respiratory physiology~3~Oxygen diffuses between alveolar air and blood according to partial-pressure differences and barrier properties.|Hemoglobin carries most oxygen in blood; circulation delivers that oxygen to tissues. Carbon dioxide moves from tissues to blood and then lungs.|Delivery depends on oxygen content and blood flow, so saturation alone cannot describe the whole process.~Ventilation, diffusion, perfusion, and delivery are separate steps.|Most oxygen is carried on hemoglobin.~Why can normal oxygen saturation coexist with reduced oxygen delivery in anemia?~Saturation describes the proportion of available hemoglobin carrying oxygen. Less hemoglobin can mean lower total oxygen content despite a normal proportion.~Map air to alveolus, blood, hemoglobin, circulation, and tissue.
breathing-control~Control of respiration and acid-base links~Respiratory physiology~3~Breathing control uses brain networks and chemical feedback to match gas exchange to body needs.|Changes in carbon dioxide and acidity influence breathing drive, alongside oxygen-related and other signals.|Changing ventilation alters carbon-dioxide removal, linking breathing to acid-base regulation.~Ventilation changes carbon-dioxide removal.|A control signal and the actual response can differ.~Why can altered breathing change blood acidity?~Carbon dioxide participates in acid-base chemistry in body fluids. Changing its removal shifts that balance.~Draw a carbon-dioxide increase followed by sensing, ventilation change, and feedback.
filtration~Glomerular filtration and tubular handling~Renal physiology~4~Kidney filters move plasma water and small solutes into nephron tubes while normally retaining blood cells and most large proteins.|Tubes reabsorb useful substances and secrete others. Final excretion reflects all three processes.|A filtered substance can largely return to blood, so filtration alone does not determine urinary loss.~Excretion = filtration minus reabsorption plus secretion for a substance.|Filtering and excreting are not synonyms.~If 100 units are filtered, 90 reabsorbed, and 5 secreted, how many are excreted?~Fifteen units: 100 minus 90 plus 5. This bookkeeping separates the contributions of each process.~Solve the balance with different numbers and label the direction of each arrow.
water-electrolytes~Water, sodium, potassium and urine concentration~Renal physiology~4~Water and dissolved ions are regulated together but not identically. Their concentrations depend on both solute amount and water amount.|ADH increases water permeability in collecting ducts, while aldosterone affects sodium and potassium handling.|The kidney's medullary concentration pattern helps support water reclamation when appropriate.~ADH primarily adjusts water handling.|Concentration can change through water or solute changes.~Why does conserving water tend to reduce urine volume?~More filtered water returns to blood rather than leaving the body, concentrating the remaining urinary solutes under suitable conditions.~Compare a water-conserving state with a water-excreting state using arrows.
acid-base~Buffers, lungs and kidneys in acid-base balance~Renal physiology~4~Buffers limit abrupt acidity changes by binding or releasing hydrogen ions.|Lungs adjust carbon dioxide relatively quickly, while kidneys adjust acid excretion and bicarbonate handling over longer periods.|A disturbance and its compensation are different processes; compensation does not automatically eliminate the cause.~Name the primary disturbance before the response.|Lungs and kidneys contribute on different time scales.~Why are buffers alone insufficient for long-term acid balance?~They redistribute or bind acid-related components but do not remove the continuing net load from the body. Excretion and gas removal remain necessary.~Make a buffer-lung-kidney chart with each role and relative time scale.
digestion~Gastrointestinal motility, secretion and absorption~Digestive physiology~5~The digestive tract moves, mixes, chemically breaks down, and absorbs food components.|Different secretions provide enzymes, acid, mucus, or bicarbonate for distinct local tasks.|Absorbed nutrients travel through blood or lymph routes; digestion in the lumen and absorption across the wall are separate steps.~Movement, digestion, and absorption answer different questions.|A secretion's location helps explain its function.~Why can adequate food intake still fail to provide adequate absorbed nutrients?~Digestion or absorption can be impaired, so the amount eaten is not necessarily the amount delivered to the body.~Trace one carbohydrate from a meal through digestion, absorption, and transport.
endocrine-reproductive~Endocrine axes, glucose control and reproduction~Endocrine and nervous systems~5~Hormones act on cells with suitable receptors. Brain-pituitary-gland axes use feedback to coordinate hormone release.|Insulin helps regulate nutrient handling after meals. Thyroid hormones influence metabolism, while reproductive hormones coordinate gonadal and uterine events.|A blood hormone value is interpreted in relation to upstream and downstream signals, timing, and target response.~Signal, receptor, and response all matter.|Feedback connects gland activity with its regulators.~Why can measuring only one hormone miss the mechanism of a disorder?~The value may reflect a primary gland change or a response to an upstream signal. Paired measurements and context distinguish these possibilities.~Draw one endocrine axis and add its feedback arrows.
nervous-senses~Reflexes, autonomic control and special senses~Endocrine and nervous systems~1~A reflex links a sensory input to a response through nervous-system circuits. It need not require conscious decision-making.|The autonomic system coordinates organ functions; sensory systems convert light, sound, or other stimuli into nerve signals.|Perception depends on receptors, pathways, and brain processing, so damage at different levels can affect the final experience.~Stimulus → receptor → pathway → processing → response.|A reflex is a circuit, not a single nerve.~Why can an intact sensory receptor coexist with impaired perception?~The pathway carrying its signal or the brain region processing it may be affected, so receptor function alone does not ensure normal perception.~Draw a simple reflex arc and a separate sensory pathway to the brain.
`;
MBBS_TOPIC_RAW.biochemistry = `
biomolecules~Water, pH and biomolecule structure~Chemical foundations~0~Water provides the main setting for cellular reactions. Charge, polarity, and acidity influence how molecules behave in it.|Carbohydrates, lipids, proteins, and nucleic acids have different structural roles and chemical properties.|Structure helps predict function: a water-avoiding region behaves differently from a charged surface in blood or a membrane.~Chemical environment affects molecular behavior.|pH is a measure of acidity, not the amount of every dissolved substance.~Why do lipids need special transport arrangements in blood?~Many lipids mix poorly with water, so transport particles package them within a water-compatible surface.~Compare the main jobs of the four large biomolecule groups.
protein-structure~Amino acids and protein structure~Chemical foundations~0~Amino acids join in a sequence to form a protein chain. Their side groups influence folding and interactions.|The folded structure creates binding sites and mechanical properties. Changing sequence or environment can change function.|Denaturation disrupts higher-level structure without necessarily cutting every peptide bond.~Sequence influences shape; shape supports function.|Loss of shape can mean loss of activity.~Why can heating change the behavior of a protein?~Heat can disrupt interactions maintaining its folded structure, altering the sites or surfaces needed for its job.~Draw sequence, folding, and a functional binding site as three linked levels.
enzymes~Enzyme action, kinetics and inhibition~Chemical foundations~0~Enzymes speed reactions by making the reaction pathway easier without being consumed overall.|Reaction rate depends on enzyme amount, substrate availability, and conditions. In the Michaelis-Menten model, Km is substrate concentration at half Vmax.|Competitive inhibition can be overcome by increasing substrate in the ideal model, leaving Vmax unchanged while apparent Km rises.~Km is a concentration; Vmax is a rate.|An inhibitor's effect depends on its mechanism.~Why does ideal competitive inhibition not reduce Vmax?~With enough substrate, competition can be overcome so the enzyme population can approach its original maximum rate.~Sketch rate against substrate with and without a competitive inhibitor.
glycolysis~Glycolysis and pyruvate metabolism~Fuel pathways~1~Glycolysis converts six-carbon glucose into two three-carbon pyruvate molecules in the cytosol.|Some steps use ATP and later steps produce it, giving a net energy transfer. Pyruvate then enters different pathways depending on cellular conditions.|Lactate formation helps regenerate a required electron carrier during continued glycolysis in relevant conditions.~Glycolysis occurs in cytosol.|Track carbon as well as ATP.~Why is pyruvate a connection point rather than only waste?~It can enter further energy pathways or serve other metabolic routes, linking glucose breakdown to the wider network.~Draw glucose to pyruvate and two possible destinations for pyruvate.
tca-etc~TCA cycle, electron transport and ATP~Fuel pathways~1~Fuel-derived acetyl-CoA enters the citric acid cycle, which transfers energy to electron carriers.|The electron transport chain uses electrons to help build a gradient across the inner mitochondrial membrane.|ATP synthesis uses the gradient. The cycle, transport chain, and ATP-forming system are connected but are not the same process.~Electron carriers connect fuel breakdown to ATP production.|A gradient stores usable potential for work.~Why can impaired electron transport reduce ATP formation even when fuel is available?~Fuel availability alone is insufficient if its energy cannot be transferred through the carrier-gradient-ATP sequence effectively.~Draw fuel, electron carriers, membrane gradient, and ATP as four stages.
glycogen~Glycogen synthesis and breakdown~Fuel pathways~1~Glycogen stores linked glucose units, especially in liver and muscle.|Liver glycogen supports blood-glucose regulation, while muscle glycogen supports local activity. Their physiological purposes differ.|Hormonal and cellular signals change the balance between building and breaking down glycogen.~Glycogenesis builds; glycogenolysis breaks down.|Same storage molecule can serve different tissue needs.~Why should liver and muscle glycogen not be treated as identical stores in every explanation?~Their tissues differ in enzyme equipment and physiological role: liver supports circulating glucose while muscle mainly uses its store locally.~Compare liver and muscle glycogen by purpose and use.
gluconeogenesis~Gluconeogenesis and the fed-fasted transition~Fuel pathways~1~When nutrient supply changes, the body shifts between storage and mobilization.|Gluconeogenesis makes glucose from non-carbohydrate starting materials, mainly in liver and also kidney. It uses energy and bypasses some irreversible glycolysis steps.|It supports glucose availability when dietary input and glycogen supply are insufficient.~Making glucose is not simply reversing every glycolysis step.|Always state the tissue and nutritional state.~Why does fasting change which pathways are emphasized?~External nutrient supply falls while tissues still need fuel, so stored fuel is mobilized and glucose can be newly produced.~Build a fed-versus-fasted pathway table with storage and supply arrows.
fat-metabolism~Fatty acids, beta-oxidation and ketone bodies~Lipid metabolism~2~Fatty acids are major fuel molecules stored largely as triglycerides. Mobilization releases them for use by tissues.|Beta-oxidation shortens fatty acids into acetyl-CoA units and generates electron carriers.|The liver can form ketone bodies from acetyl-CoA in certain metabolic states, providing a transportable fuel for other tissues.~Storage, mobilization, and oxidation are distinct steps.|Ketone production depends on metabolic context.~Why is releasing fat from storage not the same as obtaining ATP from it?~The fatty acids must still be transported into suitable pathways and oxidized, with energy then transferred through ATP-producing processes.~Trace stored triglyceride to fatty acid, acetyl-CoA, and energy pathways.
cholesterol-lipoproteins~Cholesterol and lipoprotein transport~Lipid metabolism~2~Cholesterol is used in membranes and as a starting material for several substances; it is not simply unwanted waste.|Lipoproteins transport poorly water-soluble lipids in blood. Chylomicrons, VLDL, LDL, and HDL have different compositions and roles.|Interpret particle measurements within a broader risk assessment rather than assigning one isolated value a complete health meaning.~Lipoprotein is the package; lipid is part of the cargo.|Dietary and liver-derived transport routes differ.~Why are chylomicrons prominent after fat absorption from a meal?~The intestine packages much absorbed dietary fat into these particles so it can travel through the body's fluid transport systems.~Draw separate arrows for dietary fat transport and liver-derived lipid transport.
amino-nitrogen~Amino-acid metabolism and the urea cycle~Nitrogen and genetic information~3~Amino-acid breakdown produces carbon-containing parts and nitrogen-containing groups that need different handling.|Transamination transfers amino groups; excess nitrogen is converted largely into urea in the liver.|Kidneys remove urea in urine, connecting liver metabolism with renal excretion.~Liver makes most urea; kidneys excrete it.|Protein breakdown is not only an energy question.~Why can impaired nitrogen disposal be harmful even when carbon fuel is available?~Nitrogen-containing waste, especially ammonia-related load, must be handled safely. Energy availability does not eliminate that separate task.~Draw amino acid splitting into carbon use and nitrogen-disposal routes.
heme-bilirubin~Heme metabolism and bilirubin~Nitrogen and genetic information~3~Heme is a component of hemoglobin and several other proteins. Its production and breakdown are organized pathways.|Breakdown contributes to bilirubin formation. The liver processes bilirubin and sends it toward bile excretion.|Accumulation may arise before liver processing, within the liver, or after processing if drainage is impaired.~Production, processing, and drainage are separate stages.|Jaundice is a finding with several mechanisms.~Why can jaundice occur without excessive red-cell breakdown?~Liver processing or bile drainage may be impaired, allowing bilirubin to accumulate despite ordinary production.~Draw bilirubin production, hepatic processing, and bile drainage.
vitamins~Vitamins and coenzymes~Nutrition and laboratory work~4~Many vitamins supply components or helpers used by enzymes. A small required amount can have a large functional effect.|B vitamins support diverse metabolic reactions; vitamin C helps collagen-related reactions, while vitamin D participates in mineral regulation.|Deficiency and excess have different mechanisms, and fat-soluble vitamins differ in handling from water-soluble ones.~A vitamin is not itself a complete food or energy supply.|A, D, E, and K are fat-soluble.~Why can one vitamin deficiency affect several organs?~The vitamin may support reactions used in many tissues, so one missing helper can disturb several linked functions.~Match three vitamins to a biochemical role and a tissue consequence to investigate.
minerals-nutrition~Minerals, energy balance and nutrition~Nutrition and laboratory work~4~Nutrition provides fuel, building materials, and small but essential regulatory components.|Minerals such as iron, calcium, and iodine have distinct functions, so equal amounts cannot substitute for each other.|Energy balance describes input and expenditure, while nutritional adequacy also concerns protein quality, micronutrients, absorption, and needs.~Calories and nutrient adequacy are different measures.|Intake is not always equal to absorption.~Why can a person receive enough energy but still have a deficiency?~The diet or absorption may provide inadequate amounts of a specific nutrient despite sufficient total energy.~Create an energy-protein-micronutrient checklist for evaluating a sample diet.
dna-rna~DNA replication, transcription and translation~Nitrogen and genetic information~5~DNA stores sequence information. Replication copies DNA, while transcription produces RNA from a DNA template.|Translation reads messenger RNA to build an amino-acid chain, which must fold and may undergo further processing.|Regulation controls which information is used, when, and how much product is made.~DNA → RNA → protein is a useful core route.|Not all RNA is translated into protein.~Why can cells with similar DNA perform different jobs?~They use and regulate different sets of genes, producing different proteins and functional states.~Draw the three information processes and label their starting material and product.
genetic-disorders~Mutations, inheritance and metabolic blocks~Nitrogen and genetic information~3~A sequence change can alter an enzyme, its amount, or its regulation, although not every change is harmful.|A blocked pathway may accumulate upstream substances and lack downstream products. Both effects can matter.|Inheritance patterns help describe transmission, while the actual biochemical consequence explains disease mechanisms.~Block → buildup and/or shortage.|A gene label is not a complete mechanism.~Why can replacing a missing product fail to address every consequence of a blocked pathway?~Upstream accumulation may still cause harm even if the downstream shortage is partly corrected.~Draw a three-step pathway with one blocked enzyme and predict two consequences.
laboratory~Biochemical tests, molecular methods and interpretation~Nutrition and laboratory work~5~A laboratory value is a measurement from a particular sample using a particular method.|Units, reference intervals, specimen conditions, and clinical context affect interpretation. PCR amplifies selected DNA sequences rather than detecting every possible disease.|Trends and related measures can clarify a result that is ambiguous in isolation.~Always read the units and sample context.|An abnormal number is not a diagnosis by itself.~Why can the same numeric result mean different things in different reports?~The units, method, reference interval, and specimen context may differ, changing what that number represents.~Write a five-item checklist to apply before interpreting a biochemical result.
`;
const MBBS_TOPICS = MBBS_YEARS.flatMap(year => year.subjects.flatMap(subject =>
  MBBS_TOPIC_RAW[subject].trim().split('\n').map((row,index) => {
    const fields=row.split('~');
    if(fields.length!==9) throw new Error('Invalid MBBS topic row: '+subject+' '+index);
    const [slug,title,module,parent,story,remember,question,answer,task]=fields;
    return {id:subject+'-'+slug,year:year.id,subject,order:index+1,title,module,
      lesson:subject+'-'+parent,paragraphs:story.split('|'),remember:remember.split('|'),question,answer,task};
  })
));
const MBBS_TOPIC_IDS = new Set(MBBS_TOPICS.map(topic=>topic.id));
